# CLAUDE.md template

Copy `WORKING-AGREEMENT.md`, `GOTCHAS.md` and this file into any repo, then fill
this in and save it as `CLAUDE.md` at the repo root. Claude loads `CLAUDE.md`
automatically; it does not read `README.md` unless told to. That is why the
rules belong here and not in the README.

Keep it under ~60 lines. This file is re-read on every session — length is a
recurring token cost, and a long file gets skimmed rather than followed.

Fill in every section. **"None" is a valid and useful answer** — it tells the
next session it does not need to go looking.

---

```markdown
# <repo name>

<One line: what this is and what it is for.>

Read `docs/claude/WORKING-AGREEMENT.md` before making changes.
Read `docs/claude/GOTCHAS.md` before debugging anything that looks familiar.

## Run it

<The exact commands, copy-pasteable. Install, build, test, lint, dev server.>

## Prerequisites you must check before starting

<Every env var, secret, DSN, service and external dependency needed to RUN and
TEST this repo — not just to build it. Name each one exactly.

This section is the highest-value part of the file. The most expensive failure
is a session that writes correct code, then cannot verify it because a variable
was never set, and reports "done, untested" hours in.

For each: the variable name, what it is for, where the value comes from, and
what fails if it is missing.>

| Variable | Purpose | Source | Symptom if missing |
|---|---|---|---|
| `EXAMPLE_URL` | … | … | … |

## Branch and workflow

- Active branch: <name>
- <Who else pushes here. Whether multiple Claude sessions run against it.>
- <Whether to commit directly or open a PR.>

## Hard rules

<Numbered. Only things that are non-obvious AND expensive to get wrong.
Generated files that must not be hand-edited. Formats that must not change.
Anything where the wrong choice fails silently rather than loudly.>

## Verify before you claim it works

<The exact commands that prove a change is good, and what a pass looks like.
If verification requires something a session cannot do — a real DB, a device,
your credentials — say so here, so it gets raised at the start instead of
discovered at the end.>

## Known state

<Anything currently broken, half-finished, or deliberately odd. Update it when
it changes. Prevents the next session from "fixing" something on purpose.>
```
